{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SuLvmkhbzZ4cAfhKuv3oU8","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! I have all the information needed. Let me provide a quick summary of the flag parsing patterns in this codebase.\n\n## Flag Parsing Pattern Analysis\n\nBased on examining `head`, `tail`, and `cut`, here's what I found:\n\n### 1. **Package Used: Manual Parsing (NOT the standard `flag` package)**\nAll three utilities use **custom manual parsing**, not Go's standard `flag` package. Each implements its own `parseArgs()` function.\n\n### 2. **Long Flag Handling**\nThey support both styles:\n- **`--flag value`** (separate argument)\n- **`--flag=value`** (attached with `=`)\n\nExample from **head/main.go** (lines 145-202):\n```go\ncase flag == \"--lines\":\n    if len(remaining) < 2 {\n        return 0, fmt.Errorf(\"option '--lines' requires an argument\")\n    }\n    count, err := parseLineCount(remaining[1])\n    return 2, nil  // consumed 2 args\ncase strings.HasPrefix(flag, \"--lines=\"):\n    count, err := parseLineCount(flag[len(\"--lines=\"):])\n    return 1, nil  // consumed 1 arg\n```\n\n### 3. **Combined Short Flags**\nShort flags are combined like `-abc`, processed character-by-character. Flags with values stop processing:\n\nFrom **head/main.go** (lines 204-251):\n```go\nfunc parseShortFlags(flags string, remaining []string, opts *options) (int, error) {\n    consumed := 0\n    for j := 0; j < len(flags); j++ {\n        switch flags[j] {\n        case 'n':  // flag requiring an argument\n            if rest := flags[j+1:]; rest != \"\" {\n                val = rest  // -n100 or -c100\n            } else if len(remaining) > consumed {\n                val = remaining[consumed]  // -n 100\n                consumed++\n            }\n            return consumed, nil  // STOPS processing after consuming value\n        case 'q':  // flag with no argument\n            opts.header = headerNever\n            // continues to next flag\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### 4. **General Codebase Pattern**\n\nThe pattern is **consistent across all utilities**:\n\n```\nparseArgs(args) → options + files + error\n    ├── Handle \"--\" separator\n    ├── parseLongFlag() for \"--flag\" style\n    │   ├── Use string.HasPrefix() for matching\n    │   ├── Handle \"--flag value\" (return 2)\n    │   └── Handle \"--flag=value\" (return 1)\n    ├── parseShortFlags() for \"-f\" style\n    │   ├── Process each character in the flag string\n    │   ├── Handle combined flags (-abc)\n    │   └── Return count of args consumed\n    └── Collect remaining args as files\n```\n\n**Key characteristics:**\n- Manually tracking argument consumption with integer returns\n- Support for `--` to end flag processing\n- No external libraries—all custom implementation\n- Consistent error messages (\"option requires an argument\")\n- Both short and long forms where applicable\n\nThis is a Unix utilities style: simple, predictable, no dependencies on Go's flag package."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"502a51d5-9f9f-4996-97c4-b7dffe0eee01","uuid":"765c4316-4c0c-4d92-a201-c6153f3dc690","timestamp":"2026-05-21T15:46:40.221Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"In the repo at /private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5425, look at a few cmd/ utilities that use flag parsing (like cmd/head/main.go, cmd/sort/main.go, cmd/tail/main.go, or cmd/cut/main.go) to see how they parse flags. I want to know:\n1. Do they use the standard `flag` package or manual parsing?\n2. How do they handle long flags like --head-count?\n3. How do they handle combined short/long flags?\n4. What's the general pattern for flag parsing in this codebase?\n\nShow me the relevant flag parsing code from 2-3 examples. Quick search.","agentId":"a410dd13e0fbb3d4b","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! I have all the information needed. Let me provide a quick summa