{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019Frxa3eAZkVJwChz1gkMGz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Wait, I have a problem with the range parsing. The `-i LO-HI` format uses a dash as separator, but negative numbers also start with dashes. Let me check how GNU shuf handles this... Actually, GNU shuf only supports non-negative integers for `-i`, so `LO-HI` where both are non-negative. But my SplitN with \"-\" and 2 parts would fail for ranges like \"0-10\" — wait, that would work fine: \"0\" and \"10\". But what about ranges like \"5-10\"? That splits to \"5\" and \"10\", which is correct.\n\n\nThe split approach handles the basic cases correctly since negative numbers aren't supported anyway, so I can move forward with writing the test suite.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2845,"cache_read_input_tokens":27706,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2845},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"502a51d5-9f9f-4996-97c4-b7dffe0eee01","uuid":"7fb638f3-558a-428c-8905-c17ade8a0d79"}
